    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "After MI, ACE inhibitors/ARBs reduce mortality, especially with anterior MI, LVEF ≤ 40%, HTN or diabetes; given > 24 hours after thrombolysis stabilization and may be continued long-term."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Lisinopril in acute MI (hemodynamically stable, within 24 hours): 5 mg, 5 mg after 24 hours, 10 mg after 48 hours, then 10 mg daily for at least six weeks."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Lisinopril:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " K-sparing diuretics raise hyperkalemia risk; NSAIDs raise renal-impairment risk and blunt the BP effect; lithium toxicity has been reported (monitor lithium levels); contraindicated with a neprilysin inhibitor (sacubitril); no aliskiren in diabetes."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Lisinopril overdose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " hypotension is most likely, treated with IV normal saline; lisinopril can be removed by hemodialysis."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
